-- CK-84: a constrained function whose annotation names its type through an
-- ALIAS (`Pred a` for `a -> Bool`) takes as many parameters across a module
-- boundary as inside one (checker-v2.md §12.5). The importer reads the arity
-- from the interface, whose scheme body is an `alias` term; it has to look
-- through it to the function, as the exporter's own `value_arity` does.
-- Expected `2`, `t`, `1`, `f`, `2`, `t`, `2`, `t`.
--
-- Oracle twin, run on 84e3cb1: this project with `Pred a` written out as
-- `a -> Bool` in the three annotations below and in `Main.mine` prints
-- exactly the expected lines. As written, 84e3cb1 builds with exit 0 and
-- the program throws `TypeError: isGood$2 is not a function`: `M.same` in
-- value position was lowered as a read of a constant, `M$same(eq)`, a call
-- with the element missing, instead of the closure `($p) => M$same(eq, $p)`.
pub type alias Pred a =
    a -> Bool


pub same : Pred a
    where a.eq : a, a -> Bool
same x =
    x == x


pub sameL : Pred a
    where a.eq : a, a -> Bool
sameL =
    \x -> x == x


pub sameP : Pred a
    where a.eq : a, a -> Bool
sameP =
    same
